\section{Races and Locks}


# Concurrency (1 of 2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- One source of interleaving is multiprocessor hardware
  - Modern OS, and also xv6, allow kernel execution on all CPUs in multicore
    hardware
  - Multiple CPU architecture share physical RAM, OS exploits sharing in
    maintaining kernel data structures that all CPUs read and write
- Even without multiple processors, kernel interleaves execution of code (use
  and kernel)
  - System calls interleave user process to access data structures
  - Interrupts, device handlers can modify same kernel structures as
    interruptable code

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
## Concurrency

**Concurrency** refers to the situations in which multiple instruction streams
are interleaved, due to multiprocessor parallelism, thread switching, or
interrupts.
::::
:::


# Concurrency (2 of 2)

Concurrency or interleaving raises the possibility of one thread/CPU reading a
data structure while another thread/CPU is mid-way through updating it, or even
multiple thread/CPUs updating the same data simultaneously.

Without careful design, such parallel (concurrent) access is likely to yield
incorrect results or a broken data structure.

# Kernel Design and Concurrency

- Kernels are full of concurrently-accessed data
  - For example, in xv6 two CPUs (or interleaved threads) could simultaneously
    call `kalloc()` thereby concurrently popping from the head of the free list.
  - **Pros:** like to allow for lots of concurrency as this yields increased
    performance through parallelism, thus increasing OS responsiveness
  - **Cons:** kernel designers must convince themselves of correctness of kernel
    code despite such concurrency (hard)

Strategies aimed at correctness under concurrency, and abstractions that support
them, are called **concurrency control** techniques.

# Locks

::: columns
:::: {.column width=33%}
## Lock
**Lock**: A lock provides mutual exclusion, ensuring that only one CPU/thread at
a time can hold the lock.
::::
:::: {.column width=66%}
- If associate a lock with each shared data item and code always holds
  associated lock when accessing the item, ensures only one CPU/thread at a time
- Although locks are an easy-to-understand concurrency control mechanism, the
  downside of locks is that they can limit performance, because they
  **serialize** concurrent operations.
::::
:::
      

